# HigFlow - Apptainer/Singularity image for HPC clusters
#
# Clusters usually refuse to run Docker: the daemon runs as root and any user
# who can reach it can become root on the node. Apptainer (formerly Singularity)
# runs unprivileged, keeps the user's own identity inside the container, and
# mounts $HOME and the scheduler's directories by default - which is why it is
# what shared machines allow.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This definition bootstraps from the Docker image rather than repeating its
# build steps.
#
# The repository already demonstrates what happens when one thing is described
# in two places: CMakeLists.txt and the Makefiles list different source files,
# so neither builds the whole project and two people using two build methods
# get binaries with different features. A second, hand-maintained container
# recipe would drift the same way. There is one recipe - containers/Dockerfile
# - and this file consumes it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Build, from an image in the local Docker daemon:
#
#     docker build -f containers/Dockerfile -t higflow:latest .
#     apptainer build higflow.sif containers/apptainer.def
#
# Or straight from a registry, with no Docker involved - replace the header
# below with:
#
#     Bootstrap: docker
#     From: ghcr.io/<owner>/higflow:latest
#
# Then copy the single .sif file to the cluster. It needs nothing else.

Bootstrap: docker-daemon
From: higflow:latest

%labels
    Description  HigFlow - incompressible Newtonian, viscoelastic and multiphase flow solver
    Upstream     https://github.com/antoniocastelofilho/HigFlow

%environment
    export HIGFLOW_ROOT=/opt/higflow
    export HIGTREE_DIR=/opt/higflow/higtree
    export HIGFLOW_DIR=/opt/higflow/higflow
    export PETSC_DIR=/opt/petsc
    export PETSC_ARCH=
    export PETSC_EXTRA_LIB=-lmpi
    export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/opt/libfyaml/lib/pkgconfig
    export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/petsc/lib:/opt/libfyaml/lib:${LD_LIBRARY_PATH}

%runscript
    exec /usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh "$@"

%help
    HigFlow, containerised for HPC.

    Inspect the image:

        apptainer run higflow.sif info

    Run a case. Unlike Docker, Apptainer mounts your current directory and
    $HOME by default, so no volume flag is needed:

        cd my-case
        apptainer run higflow.sif case example2d_Newt 4

    Under Slurm, let the scheduler place the ranks and give each one the
    container, rather than launching mpirun inside a single container:

        #!/bin/bash
        #SBATCH --nodes=2
        #SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=16
        #SBATCH --time=04:00:00

        module load apptainer          # or singularity, per your site
        srun apptainer exec higflow.sif \
            /opt/higflow/higflow/example2d_Newt/ns-example \
            input/example-2d.load output/run.save VTKS/run.print \
            -ksp_type bcgs -pc_type bjacobi

    One caveat worth knowing before you debug it the hard way: MPI must match.
    The image carries OpenMPI from Ubuntu 22.04, and a multi-node run needs
    that to be compatible with the host's MPI and its interconnect drivers.
    Within one node it does not matter. Across nodes, if the cluster uses
    InfiniBand or Omni-Path, bind the host MPI in instead:

        apptainer exec --bind /opt/mpi:/opt/mpi ...

    Ask the site's support what they expect - every cluster answers this
    differently, and getting it wrong shows up as a run that works on one node
    and hangs on two.
